WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.960
What is the internet?

00:00:09.983 --> 00:00:13.183
It's like a popular thing.

00:00:13.183 --> 00:00:14.583
Some satellites up there.

00:00:14.583 --> 00:00:19.143
I pictured my head with like waves of internet going to the phone.

00:00:19.143 --> 00:00:19.967
Somebody told me

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:29.951
a cloud once.

00:00:29.951 --> 00:00:34.391
the ballpoint pen or the flush toilet or the zipper.

00:00:34.391 --> 00:00:37.511
These are all things we just use every day and we don't even think about the fact that

00:00:37.511 --> 00:00:39.311
one day somebody invented them.

00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:41.855
So the internet is just like that.

00:00:41.855 --> 00:00:45.615
Many, many years ago, in the early 1970s,

00:00:45.615 --> 00:00:49.095
my partner Bob Kahn and I began working on the design

00:00:49.095 --> 00:00:49.919
of what we now call...

00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:57.279
the internet. It was a result of another experiment called the ARPANET, which stood for Advanced

00:00:57.279 --> 00:00:59.899
Research Projects Agency Network. It was a defense to

00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:02.503
Department research project.

00:01:02.503 --> 00:01:04.583
Paul Barron was trying to figure out

00:01:04.583 --> 00:01:07.463
how to build a communication system that might actually

00:01:07.463 --> 00:01:09.887
survive a nuclear attack.

00:01:09.887 --> 00:01:16.447
So he had this idea of breaking messages up into blocks and sending them as fast as possible

00:01:16.447 --> 00:01:19.087
in every possible direction through the mesh network.

00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:26.231
So we built a what eventually became a nationwide experimental packet network and it worked

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:32.015
(upbeat music)

00:01:32.015 --> 00:01:34.055
- Is anybody in charge of the internet?

00:01:34.055 --> 00:01:35.935
- The government controls it.

00:01:35.935 --> 00:01:38.055
- Elves, obviously elves.

00:01:38.055 --> 00:01:39.839
- The people who control the wifi, 'cause--

00:01:39.839 --> 00:01:41.839
No Wi-Fi, no internet.

00:01:41.839 --> 00:01:43.839
T-Mobile, um...

00:01:43.839 --> 00:01:45.839
Xfinity...

00:01:45.839 --> 00:01:46.839
Bill Gates?

00:01:46.839 --> 00:01:48.839
Bill Gates, Bill Gates.

00:01:49.823 --> 00:01:50.823
Right?

00:01:50.823 --> 00:01:53.143
The honest answer is, well, nobody.

00:01:53.143 --> 00:01:55.583
And maybe another answer is everybody.

00:01:55.583 --> 00:01:59.783
The real answer is that the internet is made up of...

00:01:59.807 --> 00:02:03.707
an incredibly large number of independently operated networks.

00:02:03.707 --> 00:02:06.947
What's interesting about the system is that it's fully distributed.

00:02:06.947 --> 00:02:09.791
There's no central control that's deciding how packets are routed.

00:02:09.791 --> 00:02:13.791
or where pieces of network are built, or even who interconnects with whom.

00:02:13.791 --> 00:02:17.791
These are all business decisions that are made independently by the operators.

00:02:17.791 --> 00:02:19.775
They are all motivated.

00:02:19.775 --> 00:02:25.615
to assure that there is end-to-end connectivity of every part of the network because the utility

00:02:25.615 --> 00:02:29.535
of the net is that any device can communicate with any other device just like you want to be able

00:02:29.759 --> 00:02:32.999
to make phone calls to any other telephone in the world.

00:02:32.999 --> 00:02:35.719
There's nothing like this that has ever been built before.

00:02:35.719 --> 00:02:39.559
The idea that what you know might be useful to somebody else

00:02:39.559 --> 00:02:39.743
or somebody else.

00:02:39.743 --> 00:02:44.343
vice versa is a very powerful motivator for sharing information.

00:02:44.343 --> 00:02:46.323
By the way, that's how science gets done.

00:02:46.323 --> 00:02:47.923
People share information.

00:02:47.923 --> 00:02:49.723
So this is an opportunity for people to share information.

00:02:49.727 --> 00:02:55.747
people to think up new applications, maybe program them as apps on a mobile phone, maybe

00:02:55.747 --> 00:02:59.707
become part of the continued growth of the infrastructure of the network.

00:02:59.711 --> 00:03:02.711
to bring it to people who don't have access to it yet,

00:03:02.711 --> 00:03:05.711
or just make use of it on a day-to-day basis.

00:03:05.711 --> 00:03:09.695
You can't escape from contact with the Internet.

00:03:09.695 --> 00:03:19.535
So why not get to know it and use it?

00:03:19.679 --> 00:03:22.259
(upbeat music)

00:03:29.663 --> 00:03:32.243
(upbeat music)

00:03:39.647 --> 00:03:42.227
(upbeat music)
